  "client_view_text": " This is an approach used by many modern databases.\nThe write-ahead log (WAL) is a critical component of modern databases Here's a part of the statement to work with:\n\n**Write-Ahead Logging**\n\nA write-ahead log (WAL) allows a database to recover from a crash by ensuring that changes to the database are written to disk before they are made visible to users. This is in contrast to a journaling file system, which writes changes to a journal before writing them to the actual file system, but does not guarantee that the changes will be written to disk before they are made visible.\n\nThe basic idea behind a write-ahead log is to record all modifications made to the database in a log file before they are applied to the database itself. This log file is written to disk before the actual database file is modified. In the event of a crash, the database can recover by replaying the log file, which contains a record of all changes made to the database before the crash.\n\nHere's how it works:\n\n1.  When a user makes a change to the database, the change is first recorded in the log file.\n2.  The log file is written to disk, ensuring that the changes are persisted.\n3.  Only after the log file has been written to disk are the changes applied to the database itself.\n4.  In the event of a crash, the database can"
